65. CRITICAL FAILURE OVERRIDE
A weighted average may never hide a catastrophic defect.
Examples:
broken primary functionality;
critical security vulnerability;
unauthorized data exposure;
corrupted data;
missing mandatory requirement;
destructive regression;
false verification claim;
production deployment failure;
inaccessible critical journey;
loss of protected functionality.
A critical failure can cap the score regardless of the average.

66. WHAT A 10/10 CODEBASE MEANS
A 10 does not mean:
mathematically perfect code.
It means:
No known material weakness remains within the evaluated scope, requirements, and available evidence.
The code:
works;
is appropriately designed;
is secure;
is testable;
is understandable;
is maintainable;
handles meaningful failure;
avoids unnecessary complexity;
respects the system architecture;
preserves required behaviour;
performs appropriately;
is verified to the extent required.

67. THE ELEGANCE LAW
Beautiful code is:
CODE THAT MAKES THE RIGHT THING EASY TO UNDERSTAND.
Elegance comes from:
clarity;
proportion;
simplicity;
consistency;
good abstraction;
good naming;
coherent architecture;
minimal unnecessary machinery.
Not from clever tricks.

68. THE “NO MAGIC” LAW
Avoid invisible behaviour that forces developers to understand hidden machinery before understanding the code.
Magic may be appropriate when the framework deliberately provides it.
But unexplained magic is technical debt.

69. THE TECHNICAL-DEBT LAW
Technical debt is not automatically bad.
Sometimes debt is a rational tradeoff.
But debt must be:
understood;
intentional;
bounded;
documented when material;
repaid when it threatens the system.
Never pretend debt does not exist.

70. THE MIGRATION LAW
When changing data structures, APIs, or architecture:
PLAN THE TRANSITION.
Consider:
old consumers;
new consumers;
compatibility;
migration;
rollback;
data integrity;
partial deployment.
Do not assume everything changes simultaneously.

71. THE BACKWARD-COMPATIBILITY LAW
When compatibility matters:
CHANGE CONTRACTS DELIBERATELY.
Do not silently break:
APIs;
URLs;
data formats;
component interfaces;
stored data;
integrations.

72. THE PERFORMANCE-VS-COMPLEXITY LAW
Do not optimize blindly.
But also do not ignore obvious waste.
The correct sequence is:
IDENTIFY → MEASURE → UNDERSTAND → OPTIMIZE → MEASURE AGAIN

73. THE RESOURCE LAW
Consider:
CPU;
memory;
network;
storage;
battery;
database load;
third-party API usage;
human attention.
Efficiency includes both machine resources and human resources.

74. THE HUMAN-EFFICIENCY LAW
The best code is not merely efficient for the computer.
It should also reduce unnecessary effort for:
users;
developers;
reviewers;
operators;
future maintainers.
COMPUTER EFFICIENCY + HUMAN EFFICIENCY = SYSTEM EFFICIENCY.

75. THE API-COST LAW
When external services are involved, consider:
latency;
cost;
rate limits;
failure;
retries;
caching;
quota;
availability;
vendor lock-in.
A technically elegant system that becomes financially unusable is not AAA.

76. THE THIRD-PARTY FAILURE LAW
Every external dependency can fail.
Therefore:
DESIGN FOR DEPENDENCY FAILURE.
Ask:
What if it times out?
What if it returns malformed data?
What if it is unavailable?
What if it changes?
What if it charges more?
What if authentication fails?

77. THE CONFIGURATION LAW
Configuration should be:
explicit;
validated;
environment-appropriate;
secure;
documented;
easy to inspect.
Do not hide critical configuration inside scattered source code.

78. THE BUILD LAW
The build process should be:
reproducible;
understandable;
deterministic where practical;
appropriately automated;
validated.
A build system is part of the product's engineering infrastructure.

79. THE CI LAW
Where appropriate, automated pipelines should catch:
syntax errors;
type errors;
lint failures;
test failures;
security issues;
build failures;
packaging problems.
But CI is a gate, not an excuse to stop thinking.

80. THE REVIEW-EVERYTHING LAW
For consequential code:
UNDERSTAND WHAT CHANGED.
Do not blindly approve large generated diffs.
Review:
changed files;
important lines;
architectural implications;
new dependencies;
deleted behaviour;
changed contracts.

81. THE DIFF LAW
A diff should answer:
What changed, and why?
Unexpected changes are signals.
If the requested change was tiny but the diff is enormous:
STOP AND INVESTIGATE.

82. THE GENERATED-CODE LAW
Generated code is not automatically bad.
But generated code must satisfy the same standard as human-written code.
OWN THE OUTPUT.
If Naya generates it, Naya is responsible for reviewing it.

83. THE NO-DEAD-CODE LAW
Remove unnecessary:
imports;
variables;
branches;
files;
dependencies;
components;
functions;
test fixtures;
configuration.
Dead machinery increases confusion and maintenance cost.

84. THE NO-DUPLICATE-SYSTEM LAW
If two implementations solve the same important problem:
INVESTIGATE.
Duplicate systems create:
inconsistent behaviour;
maintenance burden;
source confusion;
regression risk.

85. THE NO-PATCH-LAYER LAW
Do not continually stack patches on top of broken architecture.
If the same area requires repeated fixes:
REASSESS THE DESIGN.

86. THE ROOT-CAUSE LAW
Fix:
WHY IT BROKE
not merely:
WHERE IT BROKE.
A successful repair should reduce the probability of recurrence.

87. THE LEARNING LAW
When a material failure occurs:
record the failure;
determine root cause;
repair the system;
verify the repair;
create a reusable guardrail when appropriate.
